module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.verdict_measured_on_a_shared_runner_checkout_moving_under_the_dispatch

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { NoRungStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data verdict_measured_on_a_shared_runner_checkout_moving_under_the_dispatch: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "verdict_measured_on_a_shared_runner_checkout_moving_under_the_dispatch" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "**an instrument's verdict read from a REMOTE DISPATCH working tree that belongs to nobody, and which another tenant moves mid-session** (INVALID STATE: a dispatch prints its own rev-parse, runs a gate over the checkout it lands in, reports the gate's answer, and the reader takes that answer as a verdict about the revision it meant to measure. The print is honest and useless: it names the commit the checkout held at the instant of printing, not the revision the question was about, and the checkout is shared, so the tree can carry another session's UNCOMMITTED edits under a message the reporter never saw, or be re-pointed between dispatches.)",

    "THE HARM is a green read as a green about a subject it never touched, and the WORST FORM IN GENERAL IS A PASS, NOT A FAILURE: a false red gets chased, a false green is believed and forwarded as a measurement. (In THIS specimen the harm that actually FIRED was a red, not a green -- see the RUNG receipt; the ranking is about the class, not about which direction the one dispatch documented here happened to hit.) The instrument is not broken and the print is not a lie. WHAT IS MISSING IS A GUARANTEE THAT THE TREE IS THE TREE. A shared remote checkout is not a neutral environment -- which revision it holds, and whether anything is dirty on top of it, is scheduling and other tenants' state, not an input the dispatch declares.",

    "**THE RULE, stated plainly because the whole row reduces to it: a remote dispatch must CLONE FRESH at a printed SHA and must NEVER MEASURE THE SHARED CHECKOUT.** Inside one dispatch: git init, add the origin remote, git fetch --depth 1 origin <full sha>, checkout -qf FETCH_HEAD, then print git rev-parse HEAD and git status --porcelain IN THE DISPATCH and read both before trusting any verdict. Printing rev-parse alone is NOT the guard -- it is what makes the failure look verified. The guard is cloning at the SHA you asked for, so the tree cannot be someone else's.",

    "**DISTINCT FROM receipt_names_a_property_not_the_tree_it_holds_of.** That row's invalid state is an EVIDENCE BINDING that decays: the receipt named a property (first_generation_equal, zero diff) and never named the base it holds of, so a base move silently expired a receipt that had been true of a real tree. Here nothing decays and there is no stale citation: the measurement is being taken NOW, and it is taken on the WRONG TREE at the moment of taking. That row's repair is to bind a receipt to its base and re-check the base before citing; this row's repair is to construct the tree the question is about before measuring. Same family -- a verdict read as about X that was taken on not-X -- different invalid state, different check that tells them apart, different guard, which is the bar DESIGN sets for a new row.",

    "**DISTINCT FROM wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on.** That row's claim observes a per-runner HOST FACT (the runner's hostname, whether an event-log store or a device-store root exists on that machine) so its verdict is a function of which runner the job scheduled onto. Here there is no host fact and no scheduling lottery: the machine is the same every time, and what varies is the STATE OF THE SHARED CHECKOUT ON IT -- the base revision, and whose uncommitted files are layered on top. Its guard is to supply the subject at the seam; this row's guard is to clone the subject.",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-10-17, sharp-boar-42, node adhoc-07dda8cd-277, on the ctrl-build remote runner at /root/workspace/repo-root). Task: reproduce a reported required-regen drift on main. First dispatch printed `git rev-parse HEAD` = 44481f5c5657c17995925c33bccf6a3619952b32 and ran claim_executor --required-regen --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 over the checkout it landed in: rc=0, first_generation_equal=true, planned=173 executed=173 adjudicated=173 declared_divergent=1 [main.rs]. The runner's own log shows why that answered a different question: before the script ran, ctrl-build did `git clean -x -d --force`, `git checkout --force -B main 44481f5c...` and then `git apply` of SIX OF ANOTHER SESSION'S PATCHES -- src/v2/compiler/module_acquisition.dag, src/v2/compiler/reference_site_collector.dag, src/v2/compiler/resolve.dag, src/v2/std/optional_layers.dag, src/v2/std/qualified_name.dag, src/v2/test/claim/optional_declaration_identity.dag. The green was true of a tree that was not main and carried nobody's committed work; the printed rev-parse did not disclose it, because at the instant of printing the checkout really was at 44481f5. A later probe of the same workspace, after it had been re-pointed, showed HEAD=5c413a1 with ` M src/v2/compiler/resolve.dag`, ` M src/v2/std/qualified_name.dag` and a deleted claim module still on it -- the workspace belongs to no session and moves between dispatches.",

    "THE CONTRAST THAT PROVES THE GUARD, same session, same question, same day. Re-run in a dispatch that CLONED FRESH at a printed SHA: git init + fetch --depth 1 origin 5c413a1600d222374efb3eb6beb4e486fc776c8a + checkout -qf FETCH_HEAD, rev-parse printed 5c413a1600d, `git status --porcelain` EMPTY, claim_executor built from that tree -- and rc=0, first_generation_equal=true, changed_paths=[], the candidate byte-identical to the committed mirror. Repeated at the current head 7ca1c97cd38bc408984cf5e9155113be32443838 with the same empty-status tree and the same rc=0 and zero-line diff. THE REPORTED DEFECT NEVER EXISTED; what existed was a measurement taken on somebody else's tree. Two greens, one worthless and one load-bearing, and the only thing that separates them is which tree was built before the instrument ran.",

    "THE TELL, mechanical: a remote dispatch that prints rev-parse but never prints `git status --porcelain`, or prints it and does not require it empty; a dispatch whose own script did not create the checkout it measures; and any rc=0 used as evidence about a named revision without a clone at that revision in the same dispatch. The trap is bidirectional -- INFERRED, not measured here, and inferred from one case plus the mechanism: the same workspace can produce a convincing FALSE RED, which is the form that gets a repair landed. Its basis is reported separately and honestly, not as an observation of mine: the drift this node was opened to fix was reportedly 'measured' on the shared workspace with that reporter's own uncommitted src/v1/04_infer.dag edit in it, on the reporter's own account. I did not witness that run and cannot reproduce its red; what I measured is only that main is clean.",

    "**SPECIMEN (2026-10-24, quiet-carp-503, on the ctrl-build remote runner at /root/workspace/repo-root), a variant of the shared-checkout harm where the move is performed BY THE DISPATCH RUNTIME ITSELF, not by another tenant.** Task: discriminate whether a heal-candidate resolve refusal (three map_insert value-inhabitance refusals at gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_model) was caused by the candidate branch or already present on main. Every dispatch that began by checking out main INSIDE the shared workspace printed a clean resolve -- because the runner harness force-resets that checkout onto the dispatching session's branch tip (git checkout --force -B <session-branch> <session-head>) and re-applies the session's working-tree patch BEFORE the dispatched script's first command runs, so the dispatched `git checkout main` was silently undone and each clean-main verdict had actually measured the session's own tree. WHAT MAKES THIS FORM DEADLIER THAN THE TENANT-MOVE FORM: the reset happens before any dispatched command, so the script's own rev-parse --short HEAD and git status --porcelain prints (the row's mechanical tell) are taken AFTER the reset and read as the checked-out commit -- the tell is defeated from underneath, and the false green is a PASS. THE GUARD THAT WORKED, executed and discriminating: build+run inside a dispatch that CLONES FRESH from origin into a scratch path (git clone https://github.com/gunb-ai/gunbc.git /tmp/<name> && git checkout --detach <printed-sha>) and NEVER measure the shared checkout; the fresh-clone arm reproduced the refusal at the candidate and its absence at the restored arm, the in-workspace arms had produced only false clean-mains. CONSEQUENCE STATED FOR REUSE: any main-clean or baseline verdict obtained by checking a commit out inside the shared runner workspace is VOID without re-running in a fresh clone, regardless of what its own prints say.",

    "RUNG found at 1, on the executed evidence rather than on the framing the node arrived with. WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS, direction included: the harm that fired in this dispatch is a FALSE RED about main that arrived as a work item and nearly got a REPAIR LANDED -- a mirror regeneration nobody needed, which would have touched a file three other sessions were editing. That red is the dangerous direction of this class precisely because a red gets chased. WHAT THE EVIDENCE DOES NOT SHOW: that the false-green pass this dispatch also produced is what misled anyone. That run read first_generation_equal=true, meaning no drift, and it was RIGHT about its result -- main genuinely has no drift -- so it is an accidental green, a verdict reached without a tree and correct by luck, not a cause of the work item. Its defect is that nothing in it could have told a reader it was unverifiable, which is why the class is worth a row even though this particular green happened to be true. Stated the other way the rung would be a claim this dispatch did not earn. CEILING 2, mechanically preventable: every remote dispatch script that reports a verdict about a named revision begins by cloning that revision into a session-private directory and prints rev-parse AND empty status from it, and the shared checkout is never a measurement subject. NEXT TRIGGER, capability not artifact: the runner refuses -- or refuses to label as verdicts -- measurements whose script never established its tree.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on", decl_name: "wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.receipt_names_a_property_not_the_tree_it_holds_of", decl_name: "receipt_names_a_property_not_the_tree_it_holds_of"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls", decl_name: "check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.required_regen", decl_name: "RequiredRegenRunOutcome"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.regen_receipt", decl_name: "RegenReceipt"),
  ],
}
